 "decoded_text": "The function `check_sm2_c3` is intended to verify the integrity of an SM2 ciphertext by comparing a computed hash (`expected`) with the provided hash (`ct->c3.data`).\n\n1.  **Line 6**: Allocates a buffer `expected` of size `SM3_DIGEST_LEN` (32 bytes).\n2.  **Line 8**: Calls `sm3_c3_hash` to compute the hash into `expected`. This is safe assuming `sm3_c3_hash` writes exactly `SM3_DIGEST_LEN` bytes.\n3.  **Line 11**: Calls `ct_memcmp(expected, ct->c3.data, SM3_DIGEST_LEN)`.\n    *   `expected` is a local array of size 32.\n    *   `ct->c3.data` is a pointer to the C3 field in the ciphertext structure.\n    *   The length argument is `SM3_DIGEST_LEN` (32).\n\nThe problem statement states: \"Assume `parse_sm2_ciphertext()` may return a `c3` field of any length.\"\n\nIf the `c3` field in `ct` has a length less than `SM3_DIGEST_LEN` (32 bytes), then accessing `ct->c3.data` up to 32 bytes will result in an **out-of-bounds read** (buffer over-read). The function does not check `ct->c3.len` (or similar field indicating the length of the c3 data) before performing the comparison.\n\nTherefore, the vulnerability is introduced at the point where the comparison is made without verifying that the source buffer (`ct->c3.data`) is large enough to hold `SM3_DIGEST_LEN` bytes.\n\nThe line where the unsafe access occurs is line 11.\n\nAnswer: 11",
